WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.860 --> 00:00:02.194
Lorraine Kearney: One of
the biggest myths that I get

00:00:02.194 --> 00:00:03.896
from my clients is that

00:00:03.896 --> 00:00:07.900
I need to skip meals and
starve in order to lose weight.

00:00:07.900 --> 00:00:09.101
It's not true.

00:00:09.101 --> 00:00:11.003
So, if you skip meals,

00:00:11.003 --> 00:00:13.505
it's gonna have such a
negative effect on your body

00:00:13.505 --> 00:00:15.774
that when you do go to
sit down and eat,

00:00:15.774 --> 00:00:17.142
you'll probably overconsume.

00:00:17.142 --> 00:00:18.878
Narrator: That's 
Lorraine Kearney,

00:00:18.878 --> 00:00:22.581
one of three dietitians we
brought into our studios.

00:00:22.581 --> 00:00:23.482
My name is Ryan Turner.

00:00:23.482 --> 00:00:24.850
My name is Nikita Kapur.

00:00:24.850 --> 00:00:27.720
Narrator: To debunk 18 of
the most common

00:00:27.720 --> 00:00:29.188
weight-loss myths.

00:00:31.156 --> 00:00:34.560
Kearney: The biggest myth
that frustrates me the most

00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:37.296
is that all calories are
created equally.

00:00:37.296 --> 00:00:38.764
A calorie is not just a calorie.

00:00:38.764 --> 00:00:41.033
It depends on the source
of your calories,

00:00:41.033 --> 00:00:43.435
whether it's coming
from caloric-dense foods

00:00:43.435 --> 00:00:45.004
or nutritional-dense foods.

00:00:45.004 --> 00:00:46.572
Caloric-dense foods
would be more so

00:00:46.572 --> 00:00:48.007
 our cookies, our cakes.

00:00:48.007 --> 00:00:50.442
We can have a cookie
that's 100 calories,

00:00:50.442 --> 00:00:53.245
we'll eat it,
it'll digest really fast,

00:00:53.245 --> 00:00:55.281
then it's gonna spike
our blood sugar levels

00:00:55.281 --> 00:00:57.716
where, when we start
to crash again,

00:00:57.716 --> 00:00:59.084
we're gonna crave
more sugar

00:00:59.084 --> 00:01:00.519
for that energy pick-me-up.

00:01:00.519 --> 00:01:02.655
Narrator: And that can
make you gain weight.

00:01:02.655 --> 00:01:05.157
Kearney: On the other hand,
you can have a banana.

00:01:05.157 --> 00:01:06.358
Narrator: Which
is an example

00:01:06.358 --> 00:01:08.594
of a nutritionally
dense food.

00:01:08.594 --> 00:01:09.728
Kearney: I get the 
question a lot, 

00:01:09.728 --> 00:01:11.563
do bananas make you fat?

00:01:11.563 --> 00:01:13.399
Bananas do not
make you fat.

00:01:13.399 --> 00:01:15.567
Bananas, they're a great
source of potassium,

00:01:15.567 --> 00:01:16.936
but for those 100 calories

00:01:16.936 --> 00:01:19.238
you're also gonna get the
fiber and the nutrients

00:01:19.238 --> 00:01:20.973
that your body needs
in that cellular level

00:01:20.973 --> 00:01:23.309
to make sure that
you are healthy

00:01:23.309 --> 00:01:24.643
and that you're nourished.

00:01:24.643 --> 00:01:27.513
Narrator: And you definitely
need to nourish your body

00:01:27.513 --> 00:01:29.581
if you're trying to
lose weight.

00:01:29.581 --> 00:01:31.550
Kapur: When we are
restricting calories,

00:01:31.550 --> 00:01:35.020
you are restricting the
energy source of your body.

00:01:35.020 --> 00:01:37.356
You're also restricting the
energy source of your brain.

00:01:37.356 --> 00:01:38.590
And if that's happening,

00:01:38.590 --> 00:01:40.993
then, you know,
very primitive,

00:01:40.993 --> 00:01:42.261
protective mechanisms

00:01:42.261 --> 00:01:43.462
start to kick into place

00:01:43.462 --> 00:01:44.997
where your body
senses that as

00:01:44.997 --> 00:01:46.265
a physiological threat

00:01:46.265 --> 00:01:49.201
and does start to shift
your metabolic balance

00:01:49.201 --> 00:01:51.236
to burn less because
it's getting less.

00:01:51.236 --> 00:01:53.205
It's kinda like a
 budget. Right?

00:01:53.205 --> 00:01:54.673
So, if you have a paycheck

00:01:54.673 --> 00:01:56.508
 and you're running
out of funds,

00:01:56.508 --> 00:01:58.877
you're going to conserve
how much you pay

00:01:58.877 --> 00:02:00.279
till your next paycheck.

00:02:00.279 --> 00:02:01.347
Your body does the same.

00:02:01.347 --> 00:02:04.149
Your body will jump
into this protective

00:02:04.149 --> 00:02:06.385
physiological,
biological mechanism

00:02:06.385 --> 00:02:09.221
to reduce the amount
of energy you're using,

00:02:09.221 --> 00:02:12.024
which is why it is hard for
people to maintain weight.

00:02:12.024 --> 00:02:13.225
Narrator: And starving
yourself

00:02:13.225 --> 00:02:15.294
can also shrink
your muscles.

00:02:15.294 --> 00:02:15.928
Turner: You wanna
make sure that

00:02:15.928 --> 00:02:16.862
 you're not eating less

00:02:16.862 --> 00:02:20.699
than 70% of your
overall calorie needs.

00:02:20.699 --> 00:02:22.501
If you do, that's
where not only

00:02:22.501 --> 00:02:24.737
are you probably gonna
feel extremely hungry

00:02:24.737 --> 00:02:26.005
and it's gonna take you off

00:02:26.005 --> 00:02:27.506
of any goals that
you're setting,

00:02:27.506 --> 00:02:29.008
but you're probably
gonna start compromising

00:02:29.008 --> 00:02:30.209
your muscle mass as well,

00:02:30.209 --> 00:02:32.878
and that's where weight
loss is gonna be unhealthy.

00:02:32.878 --> 00:02:34.646
Narrator: But while the
amount of calories

00:02:34.646 --> 00:02:36.015
you consume matters,

00:02:36.015 --> 00:02:38.217
the timing might not.

00:02:39.184 --> 00:02:42.287
Turner: Timing your meals
is always a big question.

00:02:42.287 --> 00:02:44.289
Everyone comes to me
and they kinda smirk

00:02:44.289 --> 00:02:46.158
and they think that I'm
gonna give them a thumbs up

00:02:46.158 --> 00:02:47.226
when they say, "I don't eat

00:02:47.226 --> 00:02:49.628
after 6 o'clock or 7 o'clock."

00:02:49.628 --> 00:02:52.097
And I say, "Oh, all
right, do you enjoy that?"

00:02:52.097 --> 00:02:52.931
And they say, "No."

00:02:52.931 --> 00:02:55.067
And I say, "Well then
maybe eating after is OK."

00:02:55.067 --> 00:02:56.869
Because timing of day 

00:02:56.869 --> 00:02:59.805
is not going to
affect weight loss.

00:02:59.805 --> 00:03:01.940
Calories are what's
going to affect

00:03:01.940 --> 00:03:03.175
weight loss or body-fat loss.

00:03:03.175 --> 00:03:05.210
So, if you eat a bunch
of additional calories

00:03:05.210 --> 00:03:06.178
and you're in calorie surplus

00:03:06.178 --> 00:03:07.780
and those are coming
late at night,

00:03:07.780 --> 00:03:10.516
then that's what's causing
something like weight gain.

00:03:10.516 --> 00:03:11.650
Narrator: And what
about eating

00:03:11.650 --> 00:03:13.018
first thing in the morning?

00:03:14.019 --> 00:03:16.388
Kearney: It depends on the
body, and it depends on

00:03:16.388 --> 00:03:19.558
the person and their
relationship with food.

00:03:19.558 --> 00:03:21.927
For a lot of people,
me included,

00:03:21.927 --> 00:03:25.464
if I don't eat a meal, I
usually feel very deprived,

00:03:25.464 --> 00:03:28.567
and it's like I want to
make up for it later.

00:03:28.567 --> 00:03:29.935
If that happens,

00:03:29.935 --> 00:03:32.538
then that's when we can
add in a lot of calories.

00:03:32.538 --> 00:03:34.873
Personally, I'm a huge
advocate of breakfast.

00:03:34.873 --> 00:03:37.976
Our body runs on fuel,
and food is our fuel.

00:03:37.976 --> 00:03:39.778
So if we have our breakfast,

00:03:39.778 --> 00:03:42.081
then we feel we have
more sustained energy

00:03:42.081 --> 00:03:42.915
throughout the day.

00:03:42.915 --> 00:03:45.017
Narrator: And if you do
choose to eat breakfast,

00:03:45.017 --> 00:03:48.287
feel free to go for
that 2% yogurt.

00:03:48.287 --> 00:03:50.656
Turner: Now, fat is
incredibly necessary.

00:03:50.656 --> 00:03:52.257
We should not be
afraid of fat.

00:03:52.257 --> 00:03:53.192
We need fat in the diet.

00:03:53.192 --> 00:03:54.927
Fat's gonna be
necessary for things

00:03:54.927 --> 00:03:57.830
like absorbing nutrients,
like the fat-soluble nutrients

00:03:57.830 --> 00:04:00.999
like vitamin A and
D and E and K.

00:04:00.999 --> 00:04:02.768
And you also need to
make sure that fat,

00:04:02.768 --> 00:04:04.236
specifically cholesterol,

00:04:04.236 --> 00:04:06.238
is what's gonna help produce
things like your hormones,

00:04:06.238 --> 00:04:08.073
so things like estrogen and

00:04:08.073 --> 00:04:09.208
testosterone,
growth hormones,

00:04:09.208 --> 00:04:10.676
so we need all those
kind of things.

00:04:10.676 --> 00:04:12.244
Narrator: Not only is
fat healthy,

00:04:12.244 --> 00:04:16.615
but fat-free foods are often
loaded with sugar or salt.

00:04:16.615 --> 00:04:19.184
Kearney: So, if you
have a wholesome product

00:04:19.184 --> 00:04:20.986
and you're removing
the fat of it,

00:04:20.986 --> 00:04:23.088
it's gonna taste
completely different.

00:04:23.088 --> 00:04:24.556
You probably wouldn't
even like it.

00:04:24.556 --> 00:04:27.426
But what they're gonna
do is replace that flavor

00:04:27.426 --> 00:04:28.727
with something else,

00:04:28.727 --> 00:04:31.130
and usually it's either
sodium or sugar.

00:04:31.130 --> 00:04:34.199
So, with sugar,

00:04:34.199 --> 00:04:35.968
when we have, like, a yogurt

00:04:35.968 --> 00:04:38.036
that has the fruit
at the bottom,

00:04:38.036 --> 00:04:39.905
they're gonna have
way more sugars

00:04:39.905 --> 00:04:43.008
than if you had, like,
a 2% Greek yogurt.

00:04:43.008 --> 00:04:44.143
Narrator: And, as it
turns out,

00:04:44.143 --> 00:04:47.079
fat isn't the only nutrient
you can keep in your diet

00:04:47.079 --> 00:04:48.480
and still lose weight.

00:04:48.480 --> 00:04:50.516
Kearney: One of the biggest
myths I get about carbs

00:04:50.516 --> 00:04:54.553
is that you must omit them
from your diet to lose weight,

00:04:54.553 --> 00:04:56.555
or my body doesn't
digest them

00:04:56.555 --> 00:04:58.090
well and I have to omit them

00:04:58.090 --> 00:04:59.992
because I'd never
lose weight

00:04:59.992 --> 00:05:01.827
unless I restrict myself.

00:05:01.827 --> 00:05:02.661
It's not true.

00:05:02.661 --> 00:05:04.363
Narrator: And it's just
not sustainable.

00:05:04.363 --> 00:05:06.365
Kearney: It's almost 
impossible to have

00:05:06.365 --> 00:05:08.367
 a no-carb diet.

00:05:08.367 --> 00:05:10.869
Fruits and vegetables are
known as carbohydrates,

00:05:10.869 --> 00:05:13.572
and we must get those
for their nutrients.

00:05:13.572 --> 00:05:16.341
Why carbs have
a bad name?

00:05:16.341 --> 00:05:18.143
It's because of
the simple carbs.

00:05:18.143 --> 00:05:20.012
The carbs that you see
prepackaged

00:05:20.012 --> 00:05:21.513
 that are the cookies,

00:05:21.513 --> 00:05:23.682
the cakes, the sodas,
the potato chips.

00:05:23.682 --> 00:05:24.816
They're called
simple carbs

00:05:24.816 --> 00:05:26.952
because the chemical
structure of them

00:05:26.952 --> 00:05:30.088
is usually one to two glucose
molecules put together.

00:05:30.088 --> 00:05:32.791
So, when you have, like,
a small glucose molecule,

00:05:32.791 --> 00:05:34.193
it's easy for them
to break away.

00:05:34.193 --> 00:05:35.827
But with our complex carbs,

00:05:35.827 --> 00:05:38.797
they are really long
chains of carbon

00:05:38.797 --> 00:05:41.366
that usually are about
18-carbon long,

00:05:41.366 --> 00:05:42.734
and then, by the time
that your body

00:05:42.734 --> 00:05:43.735
starts to break it down,

00:05:43.735 --> 00:05:46.505
it's gonna take a while, and
that's exactly what we want

00:05:46.505 --> 00:05:48.974
because it helps balance
our blood sugar

00:05:48.974 --> 00:05:51.610
and also that fiber
keeps us full for longer

00:05:51.610 --> 00:05:53.278
and then also prevents
us from snacking.

00:05:53.278 --> 00:05:54.846
So, eat your carbs.

00:05:54.846 --> 00:05:56.949
Definitely eat your
carbs and eat your bread.

00:05:56.949 --> 00:05:59.451
Bread's delicious; it's
one of my favorite things.

00:05:59.451 --> 00:06:00.919
Narrator: And she has
a pro tip

00:06:00.919 --> 00:06:03.755
for finding bread with
more complex carbs.

00:06:03.755 --> 00:06:05.958
Kearney: Read your
ingredient list.

00:06:05.958 --> 00:06:10.829
With bread, a lot of those
mass-produced breads

00:06:10.829 --> 00:06:14.433
that are in the bread aisle
that are shelf-stable,

00:06:14.433 --> 00:06:15.634
they can last a month

00:06:15.634 --> 00:06:17.202
without getting
mold on them.

00:06:17.202 --> 00:06:18.604
And when you look
at the ingredient list,

00:06:18.604 --> 00:06:21.340
it's probably about
50 ingredients long.

00:06:21.340 --> 00:06:23.041
They're the ones
we wanna avoid.

00:06:23.041 --> 00:06:24.676
When you're getting bread,

00:06:24.676 --> 00:06:27.246
get the fresh bread that
comes from the local bakery,

00:06:27.246 --> 00:06:30.148
which is usually around
the deli counter area

00:06:30.148 --> 00:06:31.783
inside of grocery stores.

00:06:31.783 --> 00:06:34.519
Those will have maybe
four or five ingredients,

00:06:34.519 --> 00:06:36.388
it'll mold after two days,

00:06:36.388 --> 00:06:37.356
but you can preserve it

00:06:37.356 --> 00:06:38.557
by just putting it
into the freezer

00:06:38.557 --> 00:06:40.292
and take it out
as you need it.

00:06:40.292 --> 00:06:41.927
Narrator: And,
speaking of bread,

00:06:41.927 --> 00:06:45.230
what about going
gluten-free to lose weight?

00:06:45.230 --> 00:06:46.665
Kearney: Gluten-free
for weight loss

00:06:46.665 --> 00:06:48.734
can be a huge
marketing ploy.

00:06:48.734 --> 00:06:52.237
So, with gluten-free,
there are a lot of people

00:06:52.237 --> 00:06:54.273
that do have an
intolerance to gluten.

00:06:54.273 --> 00:06:57.109
Or they have celiac disease,

00:06:57.109 --> 00:06:59.278
which is where the body
starts attacking itself

00:06:59.278 --> 00:07:00.846
and can deteriorate
the body.

00:07:00.846 --> 00:07:02.247
Narrator: But there are
also people

00:07:02.247 --> 00:07:05.284
without these conditions
who are looking to...

00:07:05.284 --> 00:07:07.586
Kearney: Blame something
like the gluten

00:07:07.586 --> 00:07:09.521
without checking the
rest of their diet.

00:07:09.521 --> 00:07:12.190
If you are honest
with yourself,

00:07:12.190 --> 00:07:15.093
recording your food,
checking the ingredients,

00:07:15.093 --> 00:07:16.428
and then you eat the gluten

00:07:16.428 --> 00:07:19.031
and you feel the
intolerance, then great.

00:07:19.031 --> 00:07:21.833
But a lot of people will
choose to just jump in

00:07:21.833 --> 00:07:23.468
and be like, gluten's
the enemy.

00:07:23.468 --> 00:07:26.204
Narrator: So, most of us
don't need to cut out gluten

00:07:26.204 --> 00:07:28.974
or fat or carbs to
lose weight.

00:07:28.974 --> 00:07:32.311
But there are some products
better left on the shelf.

00:07:32.311 --> 00:07:34.479
Kearney: The diet sodas
are terrible,

00:07:34.479 --> 00:07:36.815
with all the additives,
preservatives in them,

00:07:36.815 --> 00:07:38.150
and the hidden sugars.

00:07:38.150 --> 00:07:41.787
A lot of the added sugars
are the synthetic sugars

00:07:41.787 --> 00:07:42.821
that are supposed
to be great

00:07:42.821 --> 00:07:44.756
because they don't
release insulin,

00:07:44.756 --> 00:07:47.526
which then doesn't cause a
spike in blood sugar levels.

00:07:47.526 --> 00:07:48.794
But, internally,

00:07:48.794 --> 00:07:52.431
if we don't stimulate
the release of insulin,

00:07:52.431 --> 00:07:55.667
those sugars, the synthetic
sugars, go to the liver,

00:07:55.667 --> 00:07:57.069
build up around the liver,

00:07:57.069 --> 00:07:58.837
hinder the functioning
of the liver,

00:07:58.837 --> 00:08:01.606
and then can lead to
nonalcoholic fatty disease.

00:08:01.606 --> 00:08:02.674
If I'm gonna have a soda,

00:08:02.674 --> 00:08:04.142
which I have once
in a blue moon,

00:08:04.142 --> 00:08:05.644
it will be the real thing.

00:08:05.644 --> 00:08:07.045
Yes, there's more sugar in it,

00:08:07.045 --> 00:08:10.582
but it's something that I
don't have on a regular basis.

00:08:10.582 --> 00:08:13.652
Narrator: Better yet,
she says, drink water.

00:08:13.652 --> 00:08:14.553
Kearney: Jazz up
your water.

00:08:14.553 --> 00:08:18.423
Add fruit to it, add some
mint or cucumber, lemon.

00:08:18.423 --> 00:08:20.759
Yes, it'll take a little bit
for your taste buds to reset,

00:08:20.759 --> 00:08:23.929
but you're getting so many
nutrients from that water,

00:08:23.929 --> 00:08:26.565
and your body
requires water

00:08:26.565 --> 00:08:28.700
for it to function
optimally.

00:08:29.000 --> 00:08:30.569
Water is one of
the six nutrients

00:08:30.569 --> 00:08:31.837
that the body needs.

00:08:31.837 --> 00:08:33.672
And when we're
dehydrated,

00:08:33.672 --> 00:08:36.408
it also mimics the
signs of hunger.

00:08:36.408 --> 00:08:39.244
So people turn to food a
lot if they're dehydrated,

00:08:39.244 --> 00:08:42.314
not realizing that
they're not hungry,

00:08:42.314 --> 00:08:44.216
it's just your body saying,

00:08:44.216 --> 00:08:45.784
"Give me some water,
I'm thirsty."

00:08:45.784 --> 00:08:47.619
Narrator: And what
about juice?

00:08:49.821 --> 00:08:51.823
Kearney: Oh, juice
cleanses. [laughs]

00:08:51.823 --> 00:08:54.459
So, juice cleanses are,
like, one of my pet peeves.

00:08:54.459 --> 00:08:56.695
If you're having a juice
every once in a while, great.

00:08:56.695 --> 00:08:58.797
You're still getting the
antioxidants out of it,

00:08:58.797 --> 00:09:00.065
you're still getting
the nutrients,

00:09:00.065 --> 00:09:01.733
but you're
removing that fiber.

00:09:01.733 --> 00:09:05.103
And fiber is key for the
body to support gut health.

00:09:05.103 --> 00:09:08.273
With a lot of juice cleanses,
they're hella expensive,

00:09:08.273 --> 00:09:11.410
and we have this belief

00:09:11.410 --> 00:09:12.844
that they're gonna be
better for our bodies

00:09:12.844 --> 00:09:15.547
or it's a cleansing
effect of our body.

00:09:15.547 --> 00:09:18.016
Realistically, what's
happening is that,

00:09:18.016 --> 00:09:19.618
when you have those
juice cleanses

00:09:19.618 --> 00:09:21.953
that are mostly coming
from, like, fruit sugars

00:09:21.953 --> 00:09:24.790
and then the
vegetable sugars,

00:09:24.790 --> 00:09:28.093
it's a high, high amount
of fructose in the body.

00:09:28.093 --> 00:09:30.996
When the body consumes
excess fructose,

00:09:30.996 --> 00:09:33.799
it has a spasming effect
of the GI tract

00:09:33.799 --> 00:09:36.368
that can lend to
the cleansing effect.

00:09:36.368 --> 00:09:39.538
So that when we
are actually having

00:09:39.538 --> 00:09:41.206
a reaction to the high

00:09:41.206 --> 00:09:43.341
amounts of fructose
in the body,

00:09:43.341 --> 00:09:44.910
people think it's the
cleansing effect

00:09:44.910 --> 00:09:46.378
because the marketing
ploys have

00:09:46.378 --> 00:09:48.146
led us to believe that way.

00:09:48.146 --> 00:09:49.848
But it's not.

00:09:49.848 --> 00:09:51.450
You would be better
cleansing your body

00:09:51.450 --> 00:09:54.786
by actually eating the
apple, eating the spinach,

00:09:54.786 --> 00:09:57.489
and eating all the other fruits
that are in that cleanse.

00:09:57.489 --> 00:09:59.257
That would be better for you

00:09:59.257 --> 00:10:02.994
because fiber is our
natural detox.

00:10:02.994 --> 00:10:05.330
What it does is it goes
through the body,

00:10:05.330 --> 00:10:08.366
picks up, like, excess
fat, metabolic waste,

00:10:08.366 --> 00:10:10.402
and help cleanse it out.

00:10:10.402 --> 00:10:13.038
Narrator: But juice cleanses
aren't the only diet fads

00:10:13.038 --> 00:10:15.106
that don't often work.

00:10:15.106 --> 00:10:16.441
Turner: Intermittent
fasting is probably

00:10:16.441 --> 00:10:17.909
a question I get all the time.

00:10:17.909 --> 00:10:19.911
It's...we can kinda put
it in that myth category.

00:10:19.911 --> 00:10:22.080
Narrator: Now, it can
restrict calories

00:10:22.080 --> 00:10:25.050
and, at least temporarily,
help you lose weight.

00:10:25.050 --> 00:10:27.352
Turner: If you're only allowed
to eat food for eight hours,

00:10:27.352 --> 00:10:29.554
that just gives someone
a lot of structure,

00:10:29.554 --> 00:10:31.523
and that can be very,
very helpful.

00:10:31.523 --> 00:10:33.291
You can only get so many
calories in your mouth

00:10:33.291 --> 00:10:34.593
in that time.

00:10:34.593 --> 00:10:35.427
On the flip side,

00:10:35.427 --> 00:10:37.395
someone can get a lot of
calories in their mouth

00:10:37.395 --> 00:10:38.363
during that time as well.

00:10:38.363 --> 00:10:40.699
So, someone can, and I've
seen many people do it,

00:10:40.699 --> 00:10:42.734
they've gained weight
through intermittent fasting.

00:10:42.734 --> 00:10:45.070
So it's not just gonna
be this quick fix;

00:10:45.070 --> 00:10:46.438
there's nothing magical to it.

00:10:46.438 --> 00:10:50.108
Narrator: And the same goes
for many popular diets.

00:10:50.108 --> 00:10:52.277
Kapur: So, one of the
common diets right now

00:10:52.277 --> 00:10:55.380
that is gaining popularity
is the ketogenic diet.

00:10:55.380 --> 00:10:57.282
So, a lot of people
who are doing that

00:10:57.282 --> 00:10:59.284
are just eliminating
carbohydrates,

00:10:59.284 --> 00:11:01.119
which is why that's
hard to sustain,

00:11:01.119 --> 00:11:02.721
because your body does

00:11:02.721 --> 00:11:04.322
need carbohydrates
for a reason.

00:11:04.322 --> 00:11:06.157
To be honest, there's
not a lot of research

00:11:06.157 --> 00:11:08.493
that's saying that that is
something that is helpful.

00:11:08.493 --> 00:11:10.896
There's maybe a lot of
research in mice models,

00:11:10.896 --> 00:11:12.564
but that hasn't 
been transcribed

00:11:12.564 --> 00:11:13.899
into human studies.

00:11:13.899 --> 00:11:16.535
Narrator: And while people
have lost weight on keto,

00:11:16.535 --> 00:11:19.037
it's often not
without side effects.

00:11:19.037 --> 00:11:22.073
Kapur: They're eliminating
whole grains and legumes,

00:11:22.073 --> 00:11:23.775
certain fruits
and vegetables,

00:11:23.775 --> 00:11:27.078
and really increasing
their fat intake,

00:11:27.078 --> 00:11:28.980
which, although
fats are important,

00:11:28.980 --> 00:11:31.049
excess of any
nutrient can cause

00:11:31.049 --> 00:11:32.751
metabolic changes
in your body

00:11:32.751 --> 00:11:35.387
that will impact your
cardiovascular health,

00:11:35.387 --> 00:11:37.622
your physical health,
your metabolic health.

00:11:37.622 --> 00:11:39.291
So, an example
would be patients

00:11:39.291 --> 00:11:41.026
that we're seeing in
the clinical setting

00:11:41.026 --> 00:11:44.129
are following ketogenic
diets, are seeing weight loss,

00:11:44.129 --> 00:11:47.098
however, are coming with
higher cholesterol markers,

00:11:47.098 --> 00:11:49.200
they're coming with
higher LDL markers,

00:11:49.200 --> 00:11:52.904
they're coming with more
irritable bowel symptoms,

00:11:52.904 --> 00:11:55.840
they're coming with more
gastrointestinal discomfort.

00:11:55.840 --> 00:11:58.476
Narrator: The truth is
there's no one tool

00:11:58.476 --> 00:12:00.812
that will make you
magically lose weight.

00:12:02.681 --> 00:12:05.617
Kapur: I think the most
prevalent concept

00:12:05.617 --> 00:12:07.586
around health these
days is biohacking,

00:12:07.586 --> 00:12:10.355
which is this idea that
you can defeat biology,

00:12:10.355 --> 00:12:14.526
you can work around your
genetic predispositions,

00:12:14.526 --> 00:12:16.628
your metabolic parameters,

00:12:16.628 --> 00:12:18.663
and that is actually not true.

00:12:18.663 --> 00:12:20.799
And the reason for
that is because

00:12:20.799 --> 00:12:22.200
you cannot defeat biology,

00:12:22.200 --> 00:12:24.402
you cannot hack hunger,

00:12:24.402 --> 00:12:27.072
you cannot hack access
to healthcare,

00:12:27.072 --> 00:12:29.274
you cannot hack motivation.

00:12:29.274 --> 00:12:33.244
And this idea that, again,
if those results are there,

00:12:33.244 --> 00:12:37.315
you're going to be able
to feel more satisfied

00:12:37.315 --> 00:12:38.416
is also not true.

00:12:38.416 --> 00:12:41.486
So, this biohacking works on
this concept and this belief

00:12:41.486 --> 00:12:45.223
that, you know, you can work
your way and fix your body,

00:12:45.223 --> 00:12:47.392
and that prescribes to
the social construct

00:12:47.392 --> 00:12:49.561
that it is up to you
to change that.

00:12:49.561 --> 00:12:53.999
Narrator: And that's also
why most diets don't work.

00:12:53.999 --> 00:12:54.899
Kapur: They're hard
to sustain,

00:12:54.899 --> 00:12:55.700
they're hard to maintain,

00:12:55.700 --> 00:12:57.302
so the results are
very temporary,

00:12:57.302 --> 00:12:59.471
which is why we go back
 to something,

00:12:59.471 --> 00:13:00.471
 trying something new.

00:13:00.472 --> 00:13:02.374
It's important to focus
on behaviors

00:13:02.374 --> 00:13:04.242
rather than outcomes.

00:13:04.242 --> 00:13:07.412
Kearney: Where you should
start is record your food.

00:13:07.412 --> 00:13:10.315
A simple food log
to lose weight

00:13:10.315 --> 00:13:12.684
is really just being
honest with yourself,

00:13:12.684 --> 00:13:15.186
identifying your foods
and the hidden ingredients

00:13:15.186 --> 00:13:17.389
that could be
contributing to excess

00:13:17.389 --> 00:13:20.091
of hundreds and hundreds
of calories per day.

00:13:20.091 --> 00:13:21.292
Take olive oil.

00:13:21.292 --> 00:13:22.594
Olive oil is great.

00:13:22.594 --> 00:13:23.662
But when we cook with it,

00:13:23.662 --> 00:13:26.164
we usually free-pour
it into a pan.

00:13:26.164 --> 00:13:30.402
Each tablespoon of olive
oil has 128 calories.

00:13:30.402 --> 00:13:34.305
Now, if you're pouring in,
like, 6, 7, 8 tablespoons

00:13:34.305 --> 00:13:35.740
with your vegetables,

00:13:35.740 --> 00:13:38.977
you're getting almost 1,000
calories that you don't need.

00:13:38.977 --> 00:13:41.780
Narrator: So, pro tip for
cooking with olive oil:

00:13:41.780 --> 00:13:45.150
Kearney: Put it in, wait
till your pan is hot,

00:13:45.150 --> 00:13:48.586
once it's hot, add 1 to 2
tablespoons of olive oil,

00:13:48.586 --> 00:13:50.989
and then add in
your vegetables.

00:13:50.989 --> 00:13:54.225
When the pan is hot enough,
it will disperse easier

00:13:54.225 --> 00:13:56.261
and then you'll use less.

00:13:56.261 --> 00:13:58.463
Also, once you put
the vegetables in,

00:13:58.463 --> 00:14:00.865
some water and moisture will
come from those vegetables

00:14:00.865 --> 00:14:02.667
and will add to the
liquid in the pan,

00:14:02.667 --> 00:14:05.003
so you actually don't
need to add excess in.

00:14:05.003 --> 00:14:06.237
Narrator: And if
you are being

00:14:06.237 --> 00:14:07.672
mindful of what you eat,

00:14:07.672 --> 00:14:10.675
that whole idea of cheating?
Kearney isn't a fan.

00:14:10.675 --> 00:14:12.410
Kearney: I don't think
there is any cheat meals.

00:14:12.410 --> 00:14:14.279
I don't like the word cheat.

00:14:14.279 --> 00:14:16.681
I think it gives it this,

00:14:16.681 --> 00:14:19.217
it gives us this, like,
higher power,

00:14:19.217 --> 00:14:23.321
like, oh, this is really bad
and I can't believe I did this,

00:14:23.321 --> 00:14:25.390
I completely fell
off the wagon.

00:14:25.390 --> 00:14:26.825
No, remove that.

00:14:26.825 --> 00:14:28.827
Because then you're
gonna want it more,

00:14:28.827 --> 00:14:30.729
you're gonna feel even
more guilty about it.

00:14:30.729 --> 00:14:33.098
If you go out and
you're socializing

00:14:33.098 --> 00:14:35.467
and you're trying out one of
New York's best restaurants

00:14:35.467 --> 00:14:39.270
that's filled with cream
and butter, enjoy it.

00:14:39.270 --> 00:14:41.439
Just try to get a
salad to start.

00:14:41.439 --> 00:14:45.910
And filling up on salad is a
great way to cut the calories,

00:14:45.910 --> 00:14:48.346
and then, have, like,
one of the appetizers

00:14:48.346 --> 00:14:50.415
that are not in line
with your health goals

00:14:50.415 --> 00:14:53.518
with your table and share,
'cause sharing is caring.

00:14:53.518 --> 00:14:55.053
Narrator: And there's
more good news.

00:14:56.354 --> 00:14:58.223
Kearney: You can still
lose weight

00:14:58.223 --> 00:15:01.226
while drinking
occasional alcohol,

00:15:01.226 --> 00:15:03.695
if you're sticking to
the cleaner foods.

00:15:03.695 --> 00:15:05.930
And by omitting all the foods

00:15:05.930 --> 00:15:07.699
that you tend to
enjoy in the past,

00:15:07.699 --> 00:15:10.502
by omitting alcohol, trying
to increase your exercise,

00:15:10.502 --> 00:15:15.340
and then doing this, like,
detox fad all in one go,

00:15:15.340 --> 00:15:18.443
it's overwhelming, and it's
setting you up for failure.

00:15:18.443 --> 00:15:20.945
So doing it in stages
and being more realistic

00:15:20.945 --> 00:15:22.514
about what you
can change now,

00:15:22.514 --> 00:15:24.549
and then work towards it.

00:15:24.549 --> 00:15:27.118
What I usually recommend
to my clients is

00:15:27.118 --> 00:15:28.720
take care of your food now.

00:15:28.720 --> 00:15:30.188
You'll understand
how your body

00:15:30.188 --> 00:15:31.422
feels when it's nourished,

00:15:31.422 --> 00:15:33.124
understand how your

00:15:33.124 --> 00:15:34.993
gut health is supposed
to be supported,

00:15:34.993 --> 00:15:36.461
and then we'll
focus on alcohol

00:15:36.461 --> 00:15:38.196
and working in
the exercise.

00:15:38.196 --> 00:15:39.264
Narrator: But the thing is,

00:15:39.264 --> 00:15:41.466
no matter how much
we care about it,

00:15:41.466 --> 00:15:43.902
weight definitely
isn't everything.

00:15:43.902 --> 00:15:45.336
Kapur: I think one of
the biggest myths

00:15:45.336 --> 00:15:47.272
around weight
loss and weight

00:15:47.272 --> 00:15:49.808
is that overweight
equals unhealthy,

00:15:49.808 --> 00:15:51.409
normal weight equals healthy,

00:15:51.409 --> 00:15:53.678
as defined by the
BMI category.

00:15:53.678 --> 00:15:56.147
BMI is a very inaccurate
measure of health

00:15:56.147 --> 00:15:59.784
because it is just looking
at your height and weight

00:15:59.784 --> 00:16:01.019
without taking into account

00:16:01.019 --> 00:16:03.788
what your metabolic
factors and parameters are,

00:16:03.788 --> 00:16:06.658
what is your physiological
health, your physical health,

00:16:06.658 --> 00:16:08.393
your sleep, your
mental health,

00:16:08.393 --> 00:16:10.195
your relationship to food.

00:16:11.596 --> 00:16:12.997
And I think it's
very important

00:16:12.997 --> 00:16:14.165
to factor those things

00:16:14.165 --> 00:16:17.268
if we really wanna define
someone as healthy.

00:16:17.268 --> 00:16:20.572
And if we're not gonna look
at it more holistically,

00:16:20.572 --> 00:16:21.506
I think what that does is

00:16:21.506 --> 00:16:23.808
it marginalizes people
in bigger bodies.

00:16:23.808 --> 00:16:26.578
Narrator: Plus, not
everyone can lose weight,

00:16:26.578 --> 00:16:29.347
even if they're putting
in the same effort.

00:16:29.347 --> 00:16:30.515
Kapur: That's a very
common myth,

00:16:30.515 --> 00:16:32.450
that everybody should have,

00:16:32.450 --> 00:16:33.918
has the same ability
to lose weight,

00:16:33.918 --> 00:16:35.620
and if everybody eats
the same way,

00:16:35.620 --> 00:16:36.721
they're gonna look
the same way,

00:16:36.721 --> 00:16:39.657
which is very untrue,
and that's incorrect.

00:16:39.657 --> 00:16:40.558
And the reason for that is

00:16:40.558 --> 00:16:42.427
I think it's important
to understand that

00:16:42.427 --> 00:16:45.396
someone's weight is a factor
of so many different things.

00:16:45.396 --> 00:16:47.298
It is so complex.

00:16:47.298 --> 00:16:49.667
All the way from your
genetic predispositions,

00:16:49.667 --> 00:16:53.738
your family history, your
past medical history,

00:16:53.738 --> 00:16:56.941
your relationship to food
as you were growing up,

00:16:56.941 --> 00:16:59.110
because not everybody
has access to food.

00:16:59.110 --> 00:17:02.013
Because health is about
inclusion, access,

00:17:02.013 --> 00:17:04.949
connection, joy,
physiological well-being,

00:17:04.949 --> 00:17:07.518
and we have to take those
factors into account.

